rtisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward Republicans sets the starting point, and no general-election polls or expert ratings are available to adjust it. Primary results in 2026 showed Trump-endorsed candidates winning in Alabama, a sign of continued Republican cohesion in the state.\n\nThe Democratic nominee would need a national environment far more favorable than any recent cycle to put Alabama in play. History offers little precedent for a close finish, as the state has not backed a Democrat for president in decades. The Republican nominee is a strong favorite, with the main open question being the size of the victory rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0488, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0924, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0912, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0912, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0824, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0865, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0895, "margin": -15.0}]}